  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAbove Average Temps This Week: The surface front and lower\ndewpoints will create an airmass gradient over the area on\nMonday. Locations west of Highway 65 will likely experience\nhighs in the upper 90s to low 100s with dews in the middle/upper 60s.\nAreas further northeast towards Rolla will experience highs in\nthe lower to middle 90s with dews in the upper 50s to lower 60s.\nThere are some CAMS and short range models showing lower temps\nand dews further west which is creating a little uncertainty.\nWe may need to extend the Extreme Heat Warning for locations \nwest of Springfield into Monday in future updates if confidence\nin hotter temps increases.\n\nEnsembles are then in strong agreement that the mid level ridge\nwill reassert itself over the area Tuesday and Wednesday. This\nwill allow for highs again to surge into the 90s to low 100s\nwith the hottest temps west of Springfield. Additional records\nwill be challenged. We will need to monitor for the potential \nfor more heat headlines. Surface low pressure across the plains\nmay set up a pressure gradient which would allow for southerly \nwind gusts of 15-20mph during the afternoons. This would create \nelevated fire conditions, especially west of Springfield and in \nthe worst drought areas. \n\nRain Chances Late Week: A stronger shortwave looks to move\nthrough the northern plains on Wednesday, sending a front into\nthe area. Rain chances have now increased into the 40-60% range\ninto Thursday with this frontal passage. Lower mid level\nheights should keep highs from reaching the record levels that\nwe have been experiencing however above average temps are still\nexpected late week. Ensemble variance then increases next\nweekend as another disturbance moves into the Rockies.",
